WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.680 line:84%
Welcome to this explainer.

2
00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.240 line:84%
If you spend your days guiding learners one-on-one

3
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.080 line:84%
through the quirks and nuances of the English language

4
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.460 line:84%
you're gonna wanna stick around.

5
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.560 line:84%
Today we're taking a look at a brand new

6
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.040 line:84%
creative teaching workspace

7
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.480 line:84%
built specifically for online English tutors

8
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.540 line:84%
It's called Atlas.

9
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.680 line:84%
We're gonna unpack the philosophy behind it,

10
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.760 line:84%
and honestly, if you're an online tutor,

11
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.140 line:84%
this is gonna feel incredibly close to home.

12
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:23.600 line:84%
Let's just dive right into

13
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.333 line:84%
the core of what this platform is all about.

14
00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:26.746 line:84%
But real quick,

15
00:00:26.746 --> 00:00:27.426 line:84%
let's acknowledge

16
00:00:27.426 --> 00:00:30.046 line:84%
the familiar reality of our daily workspace.

17
00:00:30.106 --> 00:00:31.146 line:84%
We all know this scene, right?

18
00:00:31.146 --> 00:00:31.426 line:84%
Whether

19
00:00:31.426 --> 00:00:33.546 line:84%
you've been teaching business English for 10 years

20
00:00:33.546 --> 00:00:34.506 line:84%
from your home office

21
00:00:34.506 --> 00:00:37.026 line:84%
or you're logging in from a lively hostel in Thailand,

22
00:00:37.026 --> 00:00:39.406 line:84%
your laptop screen probably looks a bit like this.

23
00:00:39.546 --> 00:00:41.186 line:84%
You've got a dozen browser tabs open,

24
00:00:41.186 --> 00:00:42.466 line:84%
a topic wheel ready to go,

25
00:00:42.466 --> 00:00:43.666 line:84%
that trusty Google Doc

26
00:00:43.666 --> 00:00:45.786 line:84%
filled with the questions that always seem to work,

27
00:00:45.786 --> 00:00:48.886 line:84%
and a PDF you definitely saved somewhere just in case.

28
00:00:48.946 --> 00:00:49.866 line:84%
It's an affectionate,

29
00:00:49.866 --> 00:00:52.486 line:84%
completely recognisable picture of how we teach.

30
00:00:52.546 --> 00:00:53.986 line:84%
It's not chaotic or incompetent.

31
00:00:53.986 --> 00:00:55.186 line:84%
It's just the very real,

32
00:00:55.186 --> 00:00:57.966 line:84%
functional reality of piecing a lesson together.

33
00:00:58.106 --> 00:00:59.306 line:84%
So the big question is

34
00:00:59.306 --> 00:01:01.846 line:84%
how Atlas actually shifts this dynamic.

35
00:01:02.106 --> 00:01:04.306 line:84%
Rather than having a million tools spread out,

36
00:01:04.306 --> 00:01:06.266 line:84%
it brings the material, the learner,

37
00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:08.966 line:84%
and your teaching history into one unified place,

38
00:01:09.226 --> 00:01:10.906 line:84%
and it operates on a really clear,

39
00:01:10.906 --> 00:01:12.300 line:84%
deliberate premise.

40
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.293 line:84%
Atlas lets tutors create precisely

41
00:01:14.293 --> 00:01:15.813 line:84%
what they wanna teach around the learner

42
00:01:15.813 --> 00:01:16.766 line:84%
in front of them.

43
00:01:16.766 --> 00:01:19.733 line:84%
Now, to be clear, this is not just a rescue tool

44
00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:21.733 line:84%
for when a lesson goes completely off the rails

45
00:01:21.733 --> 00:01:23.513 line:84%
or changes direction halfway through.

46
00:01:23.653 --> 00:01:25.573 line:84%
It is a highly creative workspace

47
00:01:25.573 --> 00:01:27.933 line:84%
built from the ground up for you to craft substantial

48
00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:29.366 line:84%
meaningful lessons.

49
00:01:29.366 --> 00:01:32.153 line:84%
All right. Section 1: Create for the learner.

50
00:01:32.213 --> 00:01:35.473 line:84%
Let's talk about generating highly specific subjects.

51
00:01:35.653 --> 00:01:38.813 line:84%
You know, one-to-one teaching is inherently personal,

52
00:01:38.813 --> 00:01:39.653 line:84%
but so often

53
00:01:39.653 --> 00:01:42.433 line:84%
the off-the-shelf material we find just isn't.

54
00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:44.733 line:84%
Here is how that creation process flows

55
00:01:44.733 --> 00:01:45.633 line:84%
in Atlas.

56
00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:48.933 line:84%
You actually don't start with a generic textbook page.

57
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:50.573 line:84%
You start with an intention.

58
00:01:50.573 --> 00:01:52.013 line:84%
That could be a professional need

59
00:01:52.013 --> 00:01:54.653 line:84%
your learner has a tricky language target you wanna hit

60
00:01:54.653 --> 00:01:57.473 line:84%
or just pure curiosity about something in the news.

61
00:01:57.573 --> 00:01:58.413 line:84%
From there,

62
00:01:58.413 --> 00:02:01.533 line:84%
Atlas generates specific high-quality subject ideas,

63
00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:04.253 line:84%
and literally in seconds, that initial starting point

64
00:02:04.253 --> 00:02:07.033 line:84%
blossoms into beautifully structured teaching material.

65
00:02:07.213 --> 00:02:08.493 line:84%
The subject suggestions

66
00:02:08.493 --> 00:02:10.453 line:84%
are where you really see the eclectic

67
00:02:10.453 --> 00:02:11.753 line:84%
depth of what you can build.

68
00:02:11.773 --> 00:02:13.413 line:84%
It's all about you and your learner

69
00:02:13.413 --> 00:02:15.913 line:84%
looking at options and choosing a direction together.

70
00:02:16.053 --> 00:02:17.773 line:84%
For example, if you're focusing on work,

71
00:02:17.773 --> 00:02:18.893 line:84%
you might pull up a subject

72
00:02:18.893 --> 00:02:21.793 line:84%
like leading a meeting when nobody agrees.

73
00:02:21.813 --> 00:02:23.773 line:84%
Or maybe you wanna go a totally different route

74
00:02:23.773 --> 00:02:26.873 line:84%
with crossing the Patagonian ice field on foot.

75
00:02:27.093 --> 00:02:28.293 line:84%
You can even tackle grammar

76
00:02:28.293 --> 00:02:29.773 line:84%
through a highly personal lens

77
00:02:29.773 --> 00:02:32.233 line:84%
like present perfect through life changes.

78
00:02:32.333 --> 00:02:33.453 line:84%
You're choosing the path together,

79
00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:34.173 line:84%
which ensures

80
00:02:34.173 --> 00:02:36.333 line:84%
the material actually matters to the person sitting

81
00:02:36.333 --> 00:02:37.553 line:84%
right in front of you.

82
00:02:37.973 --> 00:02:40.173 line:84%
And why does that choice matter so much?

83
00:02:40.173 --> 00:02:43.753 line:84%
Because genuine engagement transforms the language.

84
00:02:43.973 --> 00:02:45.413 line:84%
We aren't just looking at learner

85
00:02:45.413 --> 00:02:47.853 line:84%
engagement as some dry pedagogy lesson.

86
00:02:47.853 --> 00:02:49.133 line:84%
It is the natural,

87
00:02:49.133 --> 00:02:52.273 line:84%
obvious result of using highly relevant material.

88
00:02:52.573 --> 00:02:54.653 line:84%
When a learner is genuinely invested in the

89
00:02:54.653 --> 00:02:56.393 line:84%
conversation, they relax.

90
00:02:56.613 --> 00:02:59.453 line:84%
They stop monitoring every single word they say.

91
00:02:59.453 --> 00:03:02.073 line:84%
They take risks, they reach for better vocabulary,

92
00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:04.233 line:84%
and they try to explain what they really mean.

93
00:03:04.493 --> 00:03:07.413 line:84%
The grammar and pronunciation just develop organically

94
00:03:07.413 --> 00:03:09.473 line:84%
right there in the flow of conversation.

95
00:03:10.093 --> 00:03:11.933 line:84%
Moving right along to Section 2:

96
00:03:11.933 --> 00:03:14.293 line:84%
Shape what you teach. Okay,

97
00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:16.973 line:84%
so you have this brilliant material generated.

98
00:03:16.973 --> 00:03:19.553 line:84%
Now you need to confidently make it your own.

99
00:03:19.573 --> 00:03:20.373 line:84%
And in Atlas,

100
00:03:20.373 --> 00:03:23.593 line:84%
that ownership is visible and practically effortless.

101
00:03:23.653 --> 00:03:25.133 line:84%
Take a look here. On the left,

102
00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:27.193 line:84%
we have a generated discussion question.

103
00:03:27.333 --> 00:03:28.853 line:84%
It's solid. It works.

104
00:03:28.853 --> 00:03:31.293 line:84%
But on the right, you can see how a tutor has quickly

105
00:03:31.293 --> 00:03:33.173 line:84%
rewritten that exact question

106
00:03:33.173 --> 00:03:36.033 line:84%
to perfectly fit their specific learner's context.

107
00:03:36.173 --> 00:03:37.453 line:84%
You can adjust the framing,

108
00:03:37.453 --> 00:03:39.373 line:84%
change the length, or swap out an image

109
00:03:39.373 --> 00:03:41.193 line:84%
without it feeling like a massive chore.

110
00:03:41.293 --> 00:03:43.453 line:84%
You're simply tweaking the material around your learner

111
00:03:43.453 --> 00:03:45.633 line:84%
and your own unique teaching style.

112
00:03:46.013 --> 00:03:48.433 line:84%
Which brings us to Section 3:

113
00:03:48.453 --> 00:03:50.233 line:84%
Compass and Arcade.

114
00:03:50.493 --> 00:03:52.773 line:84%
These are the two distinct teaching environments

115
00:03:52.773 --> 00:03:55.373 line:84%
within Atlas. Once your material is ready,

116
00:03:55.373 --> 00:03:57.433 line:84%
it's time to actually teach.

117
00:03:57.613 --> 00:03:59.193 line:84%
Let's look at Compass first.

118
00:03:59.333 --> 00:04:00.666 line:84%
Think of Compass as a clean,

119
00:04:00.666 --> 00:04:02.066 line:84%
structured conversational terrain

120
00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:04.200 line:84%
for your live screen-shared lessons.

121
00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.393 line:84%
The heart of it is discussion,

122
00:04:06.613 --> 00:04:08.013 line:84%
but this isn't a rigid, linear

123
00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:10.327 line:84%
sequence of prompts that you have to march through.

124
00:04:10.327 --> 00:04:13.047 line:84%
It's flexible. It lets you and your learner spend time

125
00:04:13.047 --> 00:04:15.427 line:84%
exactly where the conversation gets productive.

126
00:04:15.527 --> 00:04:16.607 line:84%
You skip what you don't need

127
00:04:16.607 --> 00:04:18.827 line:84%
and just let the discussion develop naturally.

128
00:04:19.207 --> 00:04:21.947 line:84%
Also within Compass, you have the cultural lens.

129
00:04:22.047 --> 00:04:23.247 line:84%
This is such a great feature

130
00:04:23.247 --> 00:04:26.047 line:84%
because it visually opens a subject out into culture,

131
00:04:26.047 --> 00:04:27.867 line:84%
history, and real-world context.

132
00:04:27.887 --> 00:04:30.007 line:84%
It brings in new stories or historical moments

133
00:04:30.007 --> 00:04:31.767 line:84%
connected to whatever you're talking about.

134
00:04:31.767 --> 00:04:34.047 line:84%
It basically gives your conversation fresh,

135
00:04:34.047 --> 00:04:36.087 line:84%
unexpected material to think with,

136
00:04:36.087 --> 00:04:38.487 line:84%
and lets the learner bounce those new perspectives

137
00:04:38.487 --> 00:04:40.147 line:84%
off their own experiences.

138
00:04:40.367 --> 00:04:41.567 line:84%
But let's be honest,

139
00:04:41.567 --> 00:04:44.147 line:84%
sometimes you need a totally different energy.

140
00:04:44.287 --> 00:04:46.147 line:84%
And that's where Arcade comes in.

141
00:04:46.487 --> 00:04:48.607 line:84%
Arcade is fast. It's loose.

142
00:04:48.607 --> 00:04:50.047 line:84%
It's incredibly playful.

143
00:04:50.047 --> 00:04:52.527 line:84%
It's built completely around quick decisions,

144
00:04:52.527 --> 00:04:54.187 line:84%
instinct, and imagination.

145
00:04:54.327 --> 00:04:56.867 line:84%
There are absolutely no perfect answers here.

146
00:04:56.887 --> 00:04:59.167 line:84%
The learner just has to choose, explain,

147
00:04:59.167 --> 00:05:01.107 line:84%
defend their choice, and keep moving.

148
00:05:01.207 --> 00:05:04.447 line:84%
It pushes them to use spontaneous, reactive English,

149
00:05:04.447 --> 00:05:07.167 line:84%
which, as we know, is usually much more revealing of

150
00:05:07.167 --> 00:05:08.567 line:84%
how they actually communicate

151
00:05:08.567 --> 00:05:11.527 line:84%
under pressure. Seeing them side by side like this

152
00:05:11.527 --> 00:05:13.587 line:84%
really highlights the contrast.

153
00:05:13.687 --> 00:05:15.647 line:84%
Compass gives you this substantial,

154
00:05:15.647 --> 00:05:17.787 line:84%
deep material to explore together.

155
00:05:18.007 --> 00:05:19.487 line:84%
Arcade gives you immediate,

156
00:05:19.487 --> 00:05:21.467 line:84%
quickfire material to play with.

157
00:05:21.687 --> 00:05:22.207 line:84%
And actually,

158
00:05:22.207 --> 00:05:24.607 line:84%
Arcade is an absolute lifesaver for those tricky

159
00:05:24.607 --> 00:05:26.927 line:84%
short 15-minute lessons where you just

160
00:05:26.927 --> 00:05:29.547 line:84%
don't have time to slowly build into a deep topic.

161
00:05:29.607 --> 00:05:31.727 line:84%
It can reset the mood of a longer session

162
00:05:31.727 --> 00:05:34.127 line:84%
or stand completely on its own as a brilliant

163
00:05:34.127 --> 00:05:35.233 line:84%
mini-lesson.

164
00:05:35.266 --> 00:05:38.107 line:84%
Okay. Section 4: A relationship with memory.

165
00:05:38.487 --> 00:05:40.347 line:84%
Let's talk about the long-term payoff.

166
00:05:40.527 --> 00:05:43.907 line:84%
So we've created, shaped, and taught.

167
00:05:44.047 --> 00:05:46.067 line:84%
But what happens when the session ends?

168
00:05:46.167 --> 00:05:48.147 line:84%
Well, let me introduce you to Maya.

169
00:05:48.207 --> 00:05:49.887 line:84%
Maya is a learner from Istanbul.

170
00:05:49.887 --> 00:05:51.267 line:84%
She studies urban design.

171
00:05:51.287 --> 00:05:52.707 line:84%
She's planning a move to Berlin,

172
00:05:52.727 --> 00:05:55.267 line:84%
and she has these wonderfully specific interests:

173
00:05:55.287 --> 00:05:58.387 line:84%
Brutalist architecture, stargazing, and live music.

174
00:05:58.687 --> 00:05:59.687 line:84%
The magic here is that

175
00:05:59.687 --> 00:06:02.167 line:84%
Atlas remembers this highly specific context

176
00:06:02.167 --> 00:06:03.987 line:84%
from your very first session with her.

177
00:06:04.087 --> 00:06:06.647 line:84%
It's not just sitting in some random static notes doc.

178
00:06:06.647 --> 00:06:09.307 line:84%
It's actively woven right into the workspace.

179
00:06:09.527 --> 00:06:11.447 line:84%
When Maya logs in for her next lesson,

180
00:06:11.447 --> 00:06:13.307 line:84%
her history is right there waiting.

181
00:06:13.367 --> 00:06:14.807 line:84%
You can visually see her interests

182
00:06:14.807 --> 00:06:16.567 line:84%
connected to her past subjects,

183
00:06:16.567 --> 00:06:19.507 line:84%
like Finding Your Crowd in Berlin's Live Music Scene.

184
00:06:19.687 --> 00:06:21.007 line:84%
You've got her Arcade scores,

185
00:06:21.007 --> 00:06:21.967 line:84%
and crucially,

186
00:06:21.967 --> 00:06:24.327 line:84%
you have access to saved language from earlier

187
00:06:24.327 --> 00:06:27.267 line:84%
conversations, like the phrase 'hard to put into words.'

188
00:06:27.287 --> 00:06:29.447 line:84%
So the next lesson starts with this incredible

189
00:06:29.447 --> 00:06:30.467 line:84%
shared history.

190
00:06:30.487 --> 00:06:32.727 line:84%
It doesn't force a strict lesson plan on you,

191
00:06:32.727 --> 00:06:34.527 line:84%
but it gives you a brilliant foundation to

192
00:06:34.527 --> 00:06:35.967 line:84%
either continue a previous thread

193
00:06:35.967 --> 00:06:37.707 line:84%
or pivot to something brand new.

194
00:06:37.807 --> 00:06:40.247 line:84%
So what Atlas is doing over time is twofold,

195
00:06:40.247 --> 00:06:42.467 line:84%
and it's super powerful. Number one:

196
00:06:42.487 --> 00:06:45.107 line:84%
your own personal teaching library grows.

197
00:06:45.287 --> 00:06:46.847 line:84%
The material you create and shape

198
00:06:46.847 --> 00:06:48.747 line:84%
reflects your unique methods.

199
00:06:49.207 --> 00:06:52.287 line:84%
And number two: each learner's individual teaching history

200
00:06:52.287 --> 00:06:53.867 line:84%
builds right alongside them.

201
00:06:54.127 --> 00:06:55.967 line:84%
You're no longer just producing isolated,

202
00:06:55.967 --> 00:06:59.087 line:84%
disposable lessons. You're building a rich continuity

203
00:06:59.087 --> 00:07:02.067 line:84%
that gets more intuitive with every single session.

204
00:07:02.353 --> 00:07:05.333 line:84%
And that brings us full circle to our main through line.

205
00:07:05.633 --> 00:07:07.833 line:84%
Atlas is a shared teaching environment

206
00:07:07.833 --> 00:07:10.633 line:84%
built entirely around the relationship between you

207
00:07:10.633 --> 00:07:12.573 line:84%
and the person on the other side of the screen.

208
00:07:12.833 --> 00:07:15.133 line:84%
You create exactly what you want to teach.

209
00:07:15.193 --> 00:07:17.453 line:84%
You shape it around their needs and your style.

210
00:07:17.513 --> 00:07:19.473 line:84%
And you teach it through environments that adapt

211
00:07:19.473 --> 00:07:20.700 line:84%
the energy in the room.

212
00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.513 line:84%
Create, shape,

213
00:07:22.513 --> 00:07:23.873 line:84%
teach.

214
00:07:23.873 --> 00:07:25.393 line:84%
It really is a fundamental shift

215
00:07:25.393 --> 00:07:27.553 line:84%
from just handing out static PDFs

216
00:07:27.553 --> 00:07:29.913 line:84%
to actively operating inside a dynamic

217
00:07:29.913 --> 00:07:32.953 line:84%
teaching workspace. Which leaves us with one final

218
00:07:32.953 --> 00:07:34.113 line:84%
exciting question for you

219
00:07:34.113 --> 00:07:35.773 line:84%
as you prep for your next session:

220
00:07:35.873 --> 00:07:38.373 line:84%
What will you and your learner build first?

221
00:07:38.393 --> 00:07:40.713 line:84%
Thank you so much for joining me for this explainer,

222
00:07:40.713 --> 00:07:41.813 line:84%
and happy teaching.
